## Database Design

### appointments

```
id            SERIAL PRIMARY KEY
patient_id    VARCHAR(64)
doctor_id     VARCHAR(64)
date          DATE
time          TIME
status        VARCHAR(32)   -- confirmed | cancelled | rescheduled
language      VARCHAR(10)
booked_at     TIMESTAMP
updated_at    TIMESTAMP
```

### doctor_schedule

```
doctor_id     VARCHAR(64)
date          DATE
slot_time     TIME
is_available  BOOLEAN       -- flips false when booked
UNIQUE (doctor_id, date, slot_time)
```

Conflict prevention uses PostgreSQL `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` row locking — two patients cannot grab the same slot simultaneously even under concurrent requests.

Available slots per doctor per day: `10:00, 11:30, 14:00, 16:30`

---

## Architecture & Memory Design

### Agent State Machine

The agent uses a 7-stage state machine instead of calling the LLM on every turn:

```
IDLE → BOOK_ASK_DOCTOR → BOOK_ASK_DATE → BOOK_ASK_SLOT
     → CANCEL_CONFIRM
     → RESCHEDULE_ASK_DATE → RESCHEDULE_ASK_SLOT
```

The LLM (GPT-4o-mini) is called **once per conversation turn** at the IDLE stage for intent classification with `max_tokens: 20`. All other turns use regex extraction (~0ms). This keeps agent processing under 25ms regardless of conversation length.

### Two-Layer Memory

**Session Memory (Redis, TTL 30 min)**

- Stores current stage + partial booking data within a session
- Key: `session:{sessionId}`
- Wiped after 30 minutes of inactivity

**Persistent Memory (Redis, no TTL)**

- Stores patient's appointment history, preferred language, last doctor
- Key: `patient:profile:{patientId}`
- Never expires — survives across sessions

---

## Latency Breakdown

| Stage          | Actual           | Target      |
| -------------- | ---------------- | ----------- |
| Whisper STT    | ~1800–2200ms     | 120ms       |
| Intent LLM     | ~400–800ms       | 200ms       |
| Agent logic    | ~15–25ms         | —           |
| Tool call (DB) | ~10–30ms         | —           |
| OpenAI TTS     | ~1200–1700ms     | 100ms       |
| **Total**      | **~3500–4500ms** | **< 450ms** |

**Why the gap exists:** Whisper and OpenAI TTS are batch APIs — they process the full audio before returning. Achieving the 450ms target requires:

- **Deepgram** streaming STT (returns transcript word-by-word as user speaks)
- **ElevenLabs** streaming TTS (streams audio chunks as text is generated)
- These would bring total latency under 500ms realistically

All latency is measured and logged on every turn:

```
STT: 1923ms | AGENT: 18ms | TTS: 1441ms | TOTAL: 3382ms
```

---

## Multilingual Support

| Language | Detection Method                  | TTS Voice |
| -------- | --------------------------------- | --------- |
| English  | Unicode + LLM fallback            | alloy     |
| Hindi    | Devanagari script detection (0ms) | shimmer   |
| Tamil    | Tamil script detection (0ms)      | shimmer   |

Language preference is saved to persistent memory on first detection and reused on all subsequent sessions.

---

## Resetting for a Fresh Demo

Clear all bookings and session data:

```bash
# Clear PostgreSQL appointments and slots
docker exec -it voice-ai-agent-postgres-1 psql -U postgres -d voiceagent \
  -c "TRUNCATE appointments, doctor_schedule RESTART IDENTITY;"

# Clear Redis session and patient memory
docker exec -it voice-ai-agent-redis-1 redis-cli FLUSHALL
```

---

## Known Limitations

- `patientId` is hardcoded as `"user123"` — no auth system
- Available slots are fixed (`10:00, 11:30, 14:00, 16:30`) — not a real calendar
- Total latency ~4s vs 450ms target (Whisper + TTS bottleneck)
- Outbound campaign uses `setInterval` — not production-safe (use BullMQ or similar)
- No barge-in / interrupt handling mid-response
- No HTTPS / WSS (plain HTTP for local demo)

---

## Trade-offs

| Decision                    | Reason                                                                  |
| --------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| State machine over pure LLM | Reduces LLM calls from ~5 per turn to 1, cutting latency by ~4000ms     |
| Redis for session over DB   | Sub-millisecond reads, automatic TTL expiry, no schema needed           |
| PostgreSQL for appointments | ACID compliance, row locking for conflict prevention, queryable history |
| Whisper over streaming STT  | Simplicity — streaming STT would require significant pipeline redesign  |
| ts-node over compiled JS    | Faster dev iteration, no build step needed                              |

---

## Evaluation Criteria Coverage

| Area                                 | Weight | Implementation                                          |
| ------------------------------------ | ------ | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| Real-time voice architecture         | 20%    | WebSocket pipeline, latency logging on every turn       |
| Agent reasoning + tool orchestration | 20%    | State machine, 4 tools, LLM intent detection            |
| Memory design                        | 15%    | Redis session + persistent, PostgreSQL appointments     |
| Appointment & conflict management    | 10%    | PostgreSQL FOR UPDATE locking, alternatives on conflict |
| Multilingual handling                | 10%    | Auto-detect en/hi/ta, persisted language preference     |
| Performance optimisation             | 10%    | Single LLM call per turn, regex entity extraction       |
| Code quality & structure             | 10%    | Modular, separated concerns, TypeScript                 |
| Documentation                        | 5%     | This README                                             |

